# Formal system

A **formal system** is a language, a set of axioms and a set of rules of
inference, each fixed so exactly that a procedure can decide what is a
formula, what is an axiom and what is a correct step. Its **theorems** are
the formulas that a finite derivation ends in. In this vault it is the
concept the others were already leaning on: the cards speak of theories,
proofs, consistency and completeness, and all four are defined only inside
one.

## Description

### A concept the vault used before it had it

The discussion asked for a definition of a strange loop "as formal as a set
theory textbook", and listed among the properties wanted a system that is
"well defined formally" and "complete and consistent or at least tries to be".
The reply answered with a tuple of four components for a
[[reflective-formal-system]] and said of the first two, a language and a
theory, that they are any formal system. That was the whole account of the
concept. Everything after it was built on top: the encoding, the provability
formula, the diagonal sentence.

This card states what was assumed there.

| Part | What it fixes | In Peano arithmetic |
|---|---|---|
| Language | which strings of symbols are formulas | the formulas of first-order arithmetic |
| Axioms | which formulas are taken as given | the Peano axioms, an infinite set a procedure can recognise |
| Rules of inference | which formula may be written after which | the rules of first-order logic |
| Derivability | follows from the three above: a formula is a theorem when a finite sequence of formulas ends in it, each an axiom or the result of a rule applied to earlier ones | $T \vdash \varphi$ |

The fourth row is not a separate choice. It is what the first three define.

### Two senses of "formal"

The word is used for two things, and the discussion moved between them.

- **Formal as rigorous.** A definition is written carefully, with its terms
  defined and its hypotheses stated. The definition of a reflective formal
  system is formal in this sense. It is mathematical prose, and a reader
  judges whether an argument that uses it is correct.
- **Formal as formalised.** The language and the rules are fixed, and whether
  a given sequence of formulas is a derivation is decided by a procedure. No
  reader's judgment enters. A proof checker can do it.

The first is a property of how something is written. The second is a property
of what can be done with it. A statement in the first sense can be discussed
and cannot be derived. Only the second supports "prove it" as an operation
with a result that does not depend on who performs it.

The vault's own definitions are formal in the first sense only. The card on
the reflective formal system calls itself the most formal answer the vault
has, and it is a definition in prose about formal systems. Nothing in the
vault is derived inside one.

### Mechanical checking is the property everything else uses

The discussion's reply writes the proof relation of a theory as a formula of
arithmetic, $\operatorname{Proof}_T(p, x)$. That step is available for one
reason: whether $p$ is a proof of $x$ is decided by a procedure, and a
relation a procedure decides is one that arithmetic can represent. A
[[godel-numbering]] encodes formulas and proofs as numbers, and the encoding
has something to say about proofs only because proofs are checkable in this
way.

So the mechanical character of a formal system is not a convenience. It is
what lets the system be turned into an object the system can talk about. A
body of rigorous prose has no proof relation to encode.

It is also the reason the discussion's warning has two sides. A program that
checks proofs executes the system from outside. A theory that represents its
own proof relation holds it inside. Both depend on the checking being
mechanical, and they are still different things.

### Consistency and completeness are about derivations

The request for a system that is "complete and consistent" was corrected in
the discussion: consistency is a hypothesis of the
[[incompleteness-theorems]], and completeness is what the theorems rule out
for a consistent, effectively axiomatized, sufficiently strong system.

Both words are defined by derivability. A system is consistent when it does
not derive a formula and its negation. It is complete when, of every sentence
and its negation, it derives one. Neither can be asked of something that has
no rules of inference, because there is nothing it derives. This is why the
correction could be made exactly for arithmetic and for nothing else in the
list of examples.

### Derivable is not true

A derivation shows that a formula follows from the axioms by the rules. Truth
is a relation between a formula and an interpretation of the language, and it
is a separate matter. Two properties connect them. A system is *sound* when
everything it derives is true in the intended interpretation. It is *complete
for its semantics* when everything true in every interpretation is derivable.

The [[godel-sentence]] is the case where the two come apart inside one
theory: under the usual hypotheses it is true in the standard interpretation
of arithmetic and is not derivable. A derivation therefore never shows more
than this: if the axioms hold, so does the conclusion. That is what
[[deduction]] claims, with the rules written down.

### A formal system does not change

The discussion added a human who can add more axioms and so expand the
formalism. Adding an axiom does not change a formal system. It gives another
one, with more theorems. The choice of the new axiom is not a derivation in
either system, and that is the step the [[reflection-progression]] is built
from. A formal system is one stage of such a progression taken alone.

### What stays outside every formal system

Stating something in words and then deriving it involves a step before the
derivation: the sentence has to be turned into a formula. That step is a
[[translation]], and it is not an application of any rule of the system. No
derivation shows that the formula says what the sentence meant.

The essay on programming languages for agents meets the same limit from
the other side: proving that a program follows a specification is not proving
that the specification is what was wanted. It also names the division of
labour that follows. Executing an established formalization is one kind of
work, and a checker can do it. Discovering or revising the formalization is
another kind, and it is where the judgment is.

The translation can still be tested, though not proved. A formula can be
translated back into a sentence and compared with the original, and two
formulas written independently for one sentence can be tested for
equivalence, which is a question a prover can answer.

### The vault's own case

The claims of this vault are sentences. About two thirds of them are also
written as atoms, such as `requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)`. For a
time that was a language with a valuation: each atom was asserted, denied,
both or unsaid, and atoms were compared only when they were the same string.
Nothing was derived, so the partial logic was not a formal system.

It is one now, and a small one.

- The language is the atomic formulas, the second of the two lanes the card
  [[atom]] separates.
- The axioms are the atoms that some claim asserts, each with the address of
  its claim.
- The rules are a short list of Horn rules, held as data in the header of the
  card [[knowledge-graph]], next to the schema.
- A theorem is an atom that the rules yield from the axioms.

"Proved" in this system means derived from named claims by named rules. It
does not mean true. Most of the axioms are positions reached by argument, and
a derivation passes their uncertainty on to its conclusion. The system also
keeps a contradiction where it is found. An atom derived from some claims and
denied by another is a fact about those claims, and nothing more is concluded
from it.

## Relations

- It is the wider concept of which a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: generalizes} is the kind that encodes its own syntax.
- Its language is built from the [[atom]] {relation: uses}, the statement it gives a value to without looking inside.
- Its derivations are [[deduction]] {relation: uses} with the rules written down.
- It is what a [[godel-numbering]] encodes, and what the [[incompleteness-theorems]] are about.
- It is fixed, against a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: contrasts}, which is a sequence of formal systems.
- Putting a sentence into its language is a [[translation]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which is not one of its steps.
- Statements about what it derives are made at the meta level of [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}.
- The rules of the vault's own small formal system are held by the card [[knowledge-graph]].
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}: the exact case of a strange loop is exact because it happens inside one.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}: it marks where discussion in words ends and derivation begins.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: a requirement that is formalised can be enforced by a checker.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A formal system that carries an encoding
  of its own syntax and formulas for its own proofs. It adds two components to
  the three defined here.
- **[[deduction]].** Inference from premises to what must follow. A formal
  system is deduction with every permitted step listed in advance.
- **[[translation]].** The change of one representation into another. It is
  how a sentence enters a formal system, and the system cannot vouch for it.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last. Each member is a formal system, and the passage between them is not a
  derivation.

